# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 3 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
paying off debt | definition | Paying off debt is a question of order before it is a question of amount, because the rate decides everything about what to attack first. Two debts with different interest rates behave completely differently: the expensive one costs more for every month it is left, so clearing that first saves real money, and this is the avalanche method. The alternative, the snowball method, clears the smallest balance first because it is finished sooner, and its argument is motivational rather than financial, which is not nothing, since a plan a person actually follows beats a cheaper plan they abandon. The detail that matters is that minimum payments are usually the least the lender requires, so debt that never shrinks is usually a sign that only the minimum is being paid. A specific figure matters too: above a certain rate, prioritising repayment genuinely beats investing, and below it the reverse is true, which is why the advice is conditional rather than absolute. | debt, interest, avalanche, snowball, minimum, pay, balance | how do i pay off my debts, should i pay off debt or invest, which debt to pay first | utang | utang
budgeting | definition | A budget is a plan for where the money goes before it goes somewhere, and the useful version is a small set of percentages rather than a detailed list, because a list detailed enough to be accurate is a list nobody will maintain. The common structure is that a set share goes to needs, a smaller one to wants, and a share to savings, with the savings line being the one people move first when the month gets tight and the one they should move last. The category that surprises people is the one for irregular costs: repairs, medical, gifts and the annual fee all land in one month and belong to every month. Tracking spending for a single month is enough to show most people where the money actually goes, which is frequently not where they think, and the habit that works is a fixed transfer to savings on payday rather than whatever is left at the end, because what is left at the end is usually nothing. | budget, expenses, track, savings, wants, needs, month | how do i budget, how to start budgeting, budget percentage | badyet | badget
